    "conclusions": [
      "H-UI holds, and by more than the model predicted: the unthrottled UI took 31.9% of the CPU (mean of SHA-256, SD write and SD read) at ui_hz=full.",
      "ui_hz=2 recovers almost all of it (5.3% remains): against full, SD write +43.3%, SD read +36.6%, SHA-256 +37.7%, CRC32 +46.5%. ui_hz=0 adds a further 2.2-6.2%.",
      "One redraw costs about 26.4 ms of CPU (26.7 ms measured at 2 Hz).",
      "The optical read does not care: 1194.2 / 1192.2 / 1196.2 KiB/s at full / 2 / 0. It is drive-bound, so the UI takes nothing from it and freed CPU cannot speed it up.",
      "The census and the measured slowdown agree within 0.2 percentage points across every CPU-bound stage and pass, so the two instruments corroborate each other.",
      "The SD card showed no stalls in 8 MiB: slow=0 in the write and read latency lines of all 16 measurements; the worst single write call was 531 ms and the worst read call 646 ms.",
      "In the optical read, poll calls of 400 us and longer (the PIO transfers and the longest waits) add up to 32-39% of the read time (400 us-1.6 ms alone: 32%); the ~1.7 million short status polls account for another 24% of it, spent waiting on the drive. That is the room an overlapped design could use (docs/experiment-plan.md, Trip 6)."
